-- #1617/#1618 BYSTANDER — the new machinery is PRESENT and nothing asserted
-- here uses it.
--
-- "The feature works" is what the sibling fixtures test. This one tests the
-- question no gate normally asks: is the code that has NOTHING to do with the
-- change still correct? The change adds a new head-tag bucket (`__fun__`) and
-- makes `headTyNode` peel effect rows, both of which are consulted by EVERY
-- dispatch in the program, not only by the ones that need them.
--
-- 🚨 AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS FIXTURE COULD NOT FAIL, and an adversarial
-- reviewer falsified it by adding ONE method to its own interface. It declared
-- an arrow impl and an effect impl and then only ever asserted ORDINARY
-- dispatch — where the goal head is concrete, so the census that the change
-- actually perturbed was never consulted. The perturbed readers are the two
-- ACCEPTANCE readers of the impl-head projection, and reaching them needs a
-- shape the old fixture did not have:
--
--   * an UNDETERMINED constraint (`szc []` — nothing fixes `a`), which is what
--     `routeUndeterminedTop` resolves by COUNTING an interface's distinct impl
--     heads: one → stamp it, two or more → `T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE`, exit 1. If
--     the arrow and effect impls below were counted, this program would stop
--     COMPILING even though neither is ever dispatched to.
--   * a MULTI-PARAM interface (`Conv`) with an effect-headed sibling impl,
--     which is what `groundOneObligation` grounds by asking for the UNIQUE impl
--     at a head tag; a second match makes it give up, `b` stays undetermined,
--     and the failure surfaces as a `Display` cascade at `println`.
--
-- Both shapes are asserted below and both would go from exit 0 to exit 1 under
-- an acceptance narrowing. That is the property this fixture claims, and it can
-- now actually lose it.
--
-- Expected `(48, True, True, True)`, hand-derived slot by slot:
--   1. `unwrap (Box 41)` = 41; `sz True` = 7 via `impl Sz Bool` (NOT the arrow
--      impl, which `Bool` cannot match, and NOT the effect impl, whose head
--      peels to `Int`); 41 + 7 = 48.
--   2. `[1, 2, 3] == [1, 2, 3]` = `True` — prelude dispatch that predates all
--      of this.
--   3. `szc []` fixes nothing, so the `Sz` constraint is UNDETERMINED. `Bool`
--      is the ONLY head the acceptance census counts (an arrow and an effect
--      row are not head type constructors), so `implCountForIfaceU Sz` is 1,
--      RULE 3 does not fire, the program is ACCEPTED, and the goal resolves to
--      `impl Sz Bool` — `szc _ = 21`, so `21 == 21` = `True`.
--
--      🚨 SLOT 3 IS THE ONE SLOT WHOSE VALUE DOES **NOT** MATCH THE PRE-CHANGE
--      COMPILER, and saying so is the point rather than a caveat. Measured on
--      base `aaa43716`, this slot is `False`: with `TyFun`/`TyEffect` heads
--      projecting to `None`, both bystander impls landed in the HEADLESS
--      bucket, which matches ANY receiver — so the arrow impl answered a goal
--      it has nothing to do with and `szc []` was `55`. That is #1617 itself,
--      one shape over, and it stays `55` on base whether the arrow impl is
--      declared first or last. What must be UNCHANGED here is the ACCEPT, not
--      the value; slots 1, 2 and 4 are the byte-identical ones.
--
--      ⚠️ `impl Sz Bool` is still declared FIRST deliberately, so that the
--      value cannot be read as a declaration-order artifact.
--   4. `conv 1` grounds `b` from the UNIQUE `Conv` impl at head `Int` — the
--      effect-headed `Conv (<Stdout> Int) String` is not counted — giving
--      `impl Conv Int Bool`, so `conv 1` is `1 > 0` = `True`.
-- Slots 1, 2 and 4 print exactly what the pre-change compiler printed — that is
-- the bystander property. Slot 3's ACCEPT is also unchanged; its VALUE is not,
-- and the 🚨 on it says why. Slots 3 and 4 are the two that turn into exit 1 the
-- moment the census stops dropping the two bystander impls, which is what makes
-- this fixture able to fail at all.
interface Sz a where
  sz : a -> Int
  szc : List a -> Int

-- ⚠️ DECLARED FIRST ON PURPOSE — see slot 3 of the derivation above.
impl Sz Bool where
  sz _ = 7
  szc _ = 21

-- present, never dispatched to
impl Sz (Int -> Int) where
  sz _ = 5
  szc _ = 55

-- present, never dispatched to
impl Sz (<Stdout> Float) where
  sz _ = 6
  szc _ = 66

interface Conv a b where
  conv : a -> b

impl Conv Int Bool where
  conv n = n > 0

-- present, never dispatched to
impl Conv (<Stdout> Int) String where
  conv _ = "never"

data Box a = Box a

unwrap : Box Int -> Int
unwrap (Box n) = n

main = println (unwrap (Box 41) + sz True, [1, 2, 3] == [1, 2, 3], szc [] == 21, conv 1)
